  "narrative": "I propose a focused follow-up set after the accepted, largely validated dataset associations. The accepted effects are very large for SMARCA4 loss/SMARCA2 dependency, MSI-high/WRN dependency, and KEAP1 loss/weaker NFE2L2 dependency, while PTEN loss/stronger STAT3 dependency is now independently validated and large but smaller in absolute terms. The first purpose of these proposals is to test whether the accepted effects survive in biologically relevant, high-frequency subgroups rather than being driven by co-occurring alterations. For PTEN-STAT3, I test PTEN loss versus STAT3 dependency among TP53-intact lines. If PTEN loss associated with much more negative STAT3 dependency remains present, the association is less likely to be a mere TP53-co-alteration artifact; if absent, co-TP53 loss could be important. For SMARCA4-SMARCA2, I test among ARID1A-intact lines. Because ARID1A loss alone did not show a meaningful SMARCA2 effect and the ARID1A interaction was not promising, persistence in ARID1A-intact cells would support specificity for SMARCA4 loss. For WRN, I test MSI-high among TP53-intact cells; the accepted MSI effect is enormous, so this is a robustness test against MSI/TP53 co-mutation confounding. For NFE2L2, I test KEAP1 loss among STK11-intact cells because STK11 is a KEAP1-adjacent lung biology context and a prior STK11 interaction was small and inconclusive. The signed direction remains +1 because more positive NFE2L2 dependency scores mean weaker dependency, which was the accepted KEAP1-loss direction. Second, I test whether MYC amplification modifies the accepted SMARCA4-SMARCA2 effect. MYC-driven transcriptional and replication stress could compound chromatin-complex stress and make SMARCA2 dependency more extreme in MYC-amplified cells, but paralog specificity and co-alteration structure may make this absent; the interaction direction is registered as stronger dependency in MYC-amplified versus non-amplified cells, with uncertainty anticipated. Third, I add mechanistic main effects for underresolved outcomes. MYC amplification may increase p53-pathway stress and hence MDM2 dependency in TP53-compatible contexts, so I test stronger MDM2 dependency under MYC amplification, registered direction -1 for more negative scores. NF1 loss de-represses RAS signaling and could increase KRAS dependency even when KRAS mutation status is not directly tested; I register stronger KRAS dependency under NF1 loss, also direction -1. Fourth, I test whether unresolved receptor-tyrosine-kinase and transcription dependencies appear in cleaner co-mutation strata: ERBB2 amplification among KRAS-wild-type cells, because KRAS-mutant bypass signaling could mask ERBB2-amplification effects; RB1 loss among TP53-intact cells for POLR2A, because the overall RB1-POLR2A signal is borderline and p53-loss co-alteration could confound it; and BRAF V600E among KRAS-wild-type cells, because mutual exclusivity with KRAS mutation could make the small overall BRAF contrast uninformative. In each raw expected direction, negative means the exposed group has more negative dependency scores, hence stronger dependency, except for NFE2L2 where positive means exposed scores are less negative, hence weaker dependency. These are hypothesis refinements and broad follow-ups within the supplied lung line dataset only; none should be interpreted as causal or clinically generalizable unless further evidence accumulates.",
  "research_notes": "Round-3 explore update. Accepted/validated current dataset associations: H6 SMARCA4 loss -> stronger SMARCA2 dependency; H7 MSI-high -> stronger WRN dependency; H13 KEAP1 loss -> weaker NFE2L2 dependency; H41 PTEN loss -> stronger STAT3 dependency with independent validation. H3 EGFR remains unresolved/deferred. Newly registered priorities: (1) robustness main effects in common strata: PTEN-STAT3 among TP53-intact; SMARCA4-SMARCA2 among ARID1A-intact; MSI-high-WRN among TP53-intact; KEAP1-NFE2L2 among STK11-intact. (2) SMARCA4-SMARCA2 interaction by MYC amplification. (3) New mechanistic main effects: MYC-MDM2 stronger dependency, NF1-KRAS stronger dependency. (4) Masking/context refinements: ERBB2 in KRAS-wt, RB1-POLR2A in TP53-intact, BRAF in KRAS-wt. Existing active broad tests H14-H19/H24-H31 and H42-H47 remain useful but new accepted-effect robustness tests and MYC/p53 pathway tests should be considered high priority. All conclusions remain dataset associations, not causal or clinical generalizability claims."
